 # Human and Specific Subject Modeling
+
+
 ## Overview
-This family of methods extends static NeRF architectures to model dynamic human subjects, enabling novel view synthesis and pose extrapolation for animatable avatars [G]. It addresses the limitations of standard NeRF, which overfits to static scenes and lacks the structural priors necessary to generalize across different poses or unseen subjects [G]. By integrating parametric body models (like SMPL) or deformable mesh representations with neural radiance fields, these methods decouple geometry and appearance, allowing for high-fidelity rendering of complex human dynamics and clothing details that implicit fields alone struggle to capture [S1][S2, unofficial].
+This family of methods modifies the standard NeRF architecture to explicitly model human subjects, addressing the challenges of dynamic geometry, non-rigid deformation, and generalization to unseen poses or identities. Unlike monolithic scene representations, these methods integrate parametric human priors (such as SMPL or MANO) or part-based decompositions to constrain the learning space, enabling high-fidelity synthesis of dynamic humans from sparse or monocular inputs.
 
 ## Shared principles
+The core task of a NeRF network architecture is to learn a continuous volumetric scene representation $F_\Theta: (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) \rightarrow (\sigma, \mathbf{c})$, where $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is the spatial position, $\mathbf{d} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is the viewing direction, $\sigma$ is the volume density, and $\mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ is the view-dependent color [G]. The rendering process integrates these outputs along rays using standard volume rendering equations [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### [LISA](12036-lisa.md)
-LISA is a part-based implicit neural representation method specifically designed for modeling human hands [12036-lisa.md]. It addresses the complexity of hand geometry by approximating the hand as a collection of rigid parts, leveraging the MANO (Modeling ANd posing Object) parametric hand model [12036-lisa.md]. Unlike general body avatars, LISA explicitly handles the articulated nature of fingers and the potential for self-occlusion or object interaction through physical constraints [12036-lisa.md]. It distinguishes itself from siblings like UVA and HumanNeRF by combining MANO-based pose parameterization with Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) for shape representation and an explicit color field for appearance [12036-lisa.md].
+### LISA
+LISA is a part-based implicit neural representation method specifically designed for modeling human hands [S1][S2, unofficial]. It approximates the complex geometry of hands by decomposing them into a collection of rigid parts, leveraging the MANO (Modeling of the HAND) parametric model [S2, unofficial]. Unlike monolithic NeRF approaches, LISA distinguishes itself by using Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) for shape description and implicitly encoding MANO shape parameters via skeletal posture, while simultaneously learning a color field for realistic appearance [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### [Unified Volumetric Avatar (UVA)](13784-unified-volumetric-avatar-uva.md)
-Unified Volumetric Avatar (UVA) is a neural radiance field representation for dynamic human avatars that enables local and independent editing of both geometry and texture while supporting novel view synthesis and pose rendering [13784-unified-volumetric-avatar-uva.md]. It distinguishes itself from methods relying on pre-defined UV mapping or two-stage distillation by anchoring structured latent codes to a deformable canonical mesh, allowing direct manipulation of 3D space without 3D-to-2D projection ambiguities [13784-unified-volumetric-avatar-uva.md].
+Unified Volumetric Avatar (UVA) is a neural representation for dynamic human avatars that enables local and independent editing of both geometry and texture while supporting novel view synthesis and novel pose rendering. It distinguishes itself from UV-mapping-based methods by anchoring structured latent codes to a deformable mesh in canonical space, allowing direct 3D-to-3D manipulation, and from two-stage distillation methods by being end-to-end trainable from scratch.
 
-### [HumanNeRF](13842-humannerf.md)
-HumanNeRF is a generalizable neural representation for high-fidelity free-view synthesis of dynamic humans that generalizes to unseen subjects and camera settings using sparse RGB inputs, avoiding per-scene training through aggregated pixel-alignment features and a pose-embedded non-rigid deformation field [13842-humannerf.md]. It differs from its siblings by combining image-based rendering with NeRF-based volume rendering into a novel neural blending scheme to address artifacts and lack of high-frequency details in synthesized textures, which are common in methods relying solely on implicit texture representation [13842-humannerf.md].
+### HumanNeRF
+HumanNeRF is a neural representation for high-fidelity free-view synthesis of dynamic humans that generalizes to unseen subjects and camera settings using sparse RGB inputs, avoiding per-scene training through aggregated pixel-alignment features and a pose-embedded non-rigid deformation field [S2, unofficial]. It differs from per-scene methods like [LISA](12036-lisa.md) by learning a generalized field from a dataset, and from other generalizable methods like [Unified Volumetric Avatar (UVA)](13784-unified-volumetric-avatar-uva.md) by explicitly combining image-based rendering features with a neural radiance field via a novel appearance blending scheme to preserve high-frequency texture details [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### [HumanNeRF-SE](15640-humannerf-se.md)
-HumanNeRF-SE is a method for synthesizing novel pose images of humans from monocular video inputs by combining explicit SMPL shape priors with an implicit NeRF representation [15640-humannerf-se.md]. It solves the problem of overfitting and high computational complexity inherent in previous NeRF-based human avatars, which typically require large numbers of optimizable parameters and multi-camera data [15640-humannerf-se.md]. Unlike sibling methods such as [HumanNeRF](13842-humannerf.md) and [Unified Volumetric Avatar (UVA)](13784-unified-volumetric-avatar-uva.md) that rely on frame-level features or learned blend weights prone to overfitting, HumanNeRF-SE uses frozen SMPL blending weights for rigid deformation and point-level spatial features for non-rigid refinement, ensuring pose generalization without pose leakage [15640-humannerf-se.md].
+HumanNeRF-SE synthesizes novel pose images of humans from monocular video inputs by combining explicit SMPL shape priors with an implicit NeRF representation. It differs from sibling methods like [HumanNeRF](13842-humannerf.md) and [MonoHuman](13842-humannerf.md) by using frozen SMPL blending weights for rigid deformation and point-level spatial features for non-rigid refinement, thereby avoiding the pose leakage and overfitting associated with frame-level features or learned weight volumes.
 
 ### [Dynamic Semantic Human NeRF](21763-dynamic-semantic-human-nerf.md)
-Dynamic Semantic Human NeRF extends the radiance field framework to model dynamic human motion by learning a consistent continuous function $G_\omega$ for geometry and appearance variations across frames [21763-dynamic-semantic-human-nerf.md]. It solves the problem of reconstructing temporally coherent 3D humans from video sequences by warping observations into a canonical reference frame using the imGHUM prior [21763-dynamic-semantic-human-nerf.md]. It sets itself apart from static human NeRF variants by conditioning the neural fields on semantic latent codes (shape $\beta$, pose $\theta$, and root transformation $T$), enabling generalization to novel poses and shapes through interpolation [21763-dynamic-semantic-human-nerf.md].
+Dynamic Semantic Human NeRF extends static radiance field optimization to model dynamic human motion by learning a continuous function $G_\omega$ that maps geometry and appearance variations across frames. It differs from sibling methods by conditioning the neural radiance field on semantically meaningful latent codes from the imGHUM model (shape $\beta$, pose $\theta$, and root transformation $T$), enabling interpolation to novel poses and shapes rather than relying solely on temporal interpolation or explicit mesh tracking.
 
